# ===========================================================================
#  What this repository does not carry.
#
#  The rule, and it is the whole of the rule:
#
#      If it cannot go into a public repository, it does not LIVE in the
#      repository. It lives outside the tree and something inside the tree
#      says where.
#
#  So this file is left holding three honest categories: files a command
#  regenerates, other people's work, and `ai-config/`, which is its own
#  private repository. Patient data lives only on the cluster, under a
#  subject's phi/ and results/, ignored by the anchored rules below. An ignore
#  rule is a guard somebody can delete by accident, so board/test/tracked.py
#  and the commit-time gate (board/tutorboard/audit.py) refuse those paths too.
#
#  EACH WORKSPACE KEEPS ITS OWN .gitignore, and those are the load-bearing
#  ones. Nothing here may shadow one of those: git will not descend into a
#  directory ignored higher up, so a rule here for a directory a workspace
#  re-includes would make every deeper `!` line there unreachable. That is
#  why this file names no directory a workspace re-includes.
# ===========================================================================

# ---- build products, every language in here ----
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
node_modules/
# A workspace's environment, which `uv sync` builds from its uv.lock.
.venv/
*.log

# Lean's compiled Mathlib: 14 GB across 150,582 files, and `lake build`
# recreates all of it. It never leaves the machine.
.lake/
build/
# Not output: the board's builder keeps its pandoc filter here.
!/board/tutorboard/build/

# ---- LaTeX, which writes eight files beside every one it is asked for ----
*.aux
*.nav
*.snm
*.toc
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.vrb
*.out
# Built PDFs, where documents are built. A PDF already tracked stays tracked.
/courses/**/*.pdf
/projects/*/docs/**/*.pdf
/projects/*/homework/**/*.pdf
# A marked copy: ink burned into a copy of a PDF, beside it (`burn.py`).
*-marked.pdf
*-marked-[0-9]*.pdf

# ---- the relay's own state, on the cluster ----
# The pass's lock and `state.json` at the root; in each subject the wrapped
# job scripts, their exit codes, the job registry, the claims and the Colibri
# queue (`jobs.py`). A log can print anything, so none of it is tracked; a
# report under `relay/reports/` is what crosses.
**/relay/state/
/relay/state.json
/relay/.lock*

# ---- the meeting deck: projects/Meetings/docs/meeting/ ----
# `meeting.tex` and doc.json are tracked. The brief quotes commits and
# sessions, the figures are copies of results, and the sidecar and the marks'
# pictures are rewritten on every build: none of them is tracked.
/projects/Meetings/docs/meeting/_brief.*
/projects/Meetings/docs/meeting/_provenance.json
/projects/Meetings/docs/meeting/figures/
/projects/Meetings/docs/meeting/marks/
# An older machine's root pointer to the deck. Nothing writes it.
/meetings/

# ---- other people's published papers ----
# The citation libraries hold PDFs whose copyright belongs to their authors.
# The INDEXES are tracked -- README.md, CITATION_MAP.md, the role-grouped
# manifests -- so a clone arrives with the bibliography described but not
# carried. Anything that grows a reference shelf gets the same treatment.
**/references/*.pdf

# ---- editor and OS cruft ----
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*~
.*.swp
.vscode/
# The NFS client's placeholder for a file deleted while still open. It is
# nobody's work and it vanishes on its own; a commit carrying one carries a
# name that is gone a minute later.
.nfs*

# ---- one vendor's config directory, none of which is this repository's ----
# NOTHING under an assistant's own directory is tracked here, and the test is
# that deleting every one of them costs `bash ai-config/scripts/install.sh` and
# nothing else.
#
# A workspace's permission allowlist -- which commands may run unattended -- is
# a fact about the workspace, and it lives in `ai-config` as
# `workspaces/*.allow`, naming no vendor. `install.sh` renders it into the
# assistant's USER-LEVEL settings, outside this tree, so nothing installs
# anything here. The rule below is therefore absolute rather than a filter: a
# directory named for an assistant is this machine's own state, never anything
# the repository needs.
#
# The same rule covers a workflow, a subagent, a slash command and a skill,
# which are logic written against one provider's API and go stale at its next
# release. A job worth keeping is kept as a specification any assistant can
# execute, and the harness is regenerated from it.
#
# `**/` on purpose: a pattern with a slash in it is anchored to the directory
# its .gitignore sits in, so `.claude/` written HERE would match only the one
# at the repository root and silently miss the nine inside the workspaces --
# which are the only ones that actually exist.
**/.claude/

# ---- the one directory that is its own private repository ----
# `ai-config/` holds the operating contract every assistant reads, the PHI
# guard, and each vendor's settings files, which name real paths on the lab's
# storage. It lives INSIDE this tree, so one directory is the whole of the
# work, and is ignored here, so a public repository never carries a byte of it.
/ai-config/

# ---- a credential dropped in the tree, which is a thing that happens ----
# A key belongs in `~/.config/tutor-board/keys.env`, machine-local and outside
# this repository -- README.md's Mac setup says so. It still gets
# dropped here first, because that is where the terminal already is, and this
# repository is public. So the shapes a key file is given are refused by name,
# unanchored, so a drop at any depth is caught rather than only one at the root.
#
# THIS IS THE FIRST OF TWO GUARDS AND IT IS THE WEAKER ONE. A pattern is right
# until somebody adds a file with `git add -f` or edits this list. The other is
# the rule that makes the PHI arrangement safe: `board/test/tracked.py` asks GIT
# ITSELF what it can see, on every run of the suite, and refuses these same
# shapes there.
*-api-key
*_api_key
*.key
keys.env
.env

# ---- what stays on the Mac, and what belongs to somebody else ----
# Sessions, document ink and materials (third-party or uploaded files) are
# never tracked. phi/ and results/ never leave the machine they are on. A
# course's textbook, chapter readings, lecture decks and assignment sheets are
# other people's work. board/tutorboard/audit.py refuses the same shapes at
# commit time, which is the guard a `git add -f` cannot walk past.
/sessions/
**/.ink/
**/materials/
/projects/*/phi/
/projects/*/results/
/courses/*/phi/
/courses/*/textbook/
/courses/*/chapters/*/reading/
/courses/*/chapters/*/lectures/
/courses/*/**/assignment*

# A subject's live/ is an old per-subject board's session state (turns, answers,
# mission trails). Sessions live in /sessions/ now; a machine that still holds
# one keeps it invisible here.
/courses/*/live/
/projects/*/live/

# ---- the legacy subject roots ----
# research/ and practice/ merged into projects/. A machine that has not yet run
# move-residue.sh still holds ignored residue (.venv, phi/, results/, .env) at
# the old paths after a pull; these keep all of it invisible to git.
/research/
/practice/
